1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:02,618
Does the evidence address the question?.

2
00:00:02,921 --> 00:00:06,461
Are comparisons and independent checks included?.

3
00:00:06,763 --> 00:00:11,180
Are alternatives and unavailable-material contingencies considered?.

4
00:00:11,487 --> 00:00:16,460
Focus less on whether you agree with a proposed explanation and more on whether its testing

5
00:00:16,474 --> 00:00:18,112
procedure is clear.

6
00:00:18,566 --> 00:00:22,204
Ask for one observation that could change the conclusion.

7
00:00:22,658 --> 00:00:28,584
If working alone, return to the draft later and review it using the same questions.

8
00:00:29,387 --> 00:00:34,625
The syllabus indicates 2–4 A4 pages and at least three references.

9
00:00:35,087 --> 00:00:39,712
Connect question, sources, methods, discussion and limits.

10
00:00:40,166 --> 00:00:44,495
Distinguish a proposal and expectations from completed results.

11
00:00:44,955 --> 00:00:49,712
The report should let a reader follow the reasoning from the question to the conclusion.

12
00:00:50,166 --> 00:00:55,139
If measurements have not been made, present the materials and procedures as a proposal

13
00:00:55,152 --> 00:00:57,567
rather than as completed findings.

14
00:00:58,021 --> 00:01:03,231
The syllabus requirements guide the format; this slide does not introduce additional grading

15
00:01:03,245 --> 00:01:05,524
weights or deadlines.

16
00:01:06,327 --> 00:01:10,045
State the conclusion supported by the available evidence.

17
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:14,013
Identify remaining alternatives and uncertainty.

18
00:01:14,474 --> 00:01:18,270
Name one further observation that could change the conclusion.

19
00:01:18,724 --> 00:01:23,448
Stating limits clarifies what has been learned and what remains unresolved.

20
00:01:23,908 --> 00:01:29,119
A useful conclusion identifies the evidence it depends on and the alternatives it has not

21
00:01:29,132 --> 00:01:30,303
excluded.

22
00:01:30,763 --> 00:01:36,224
Naming one specific additional observation turns those limits into a practical next research

23
00:01:36,237 --> 00:01:37,664
step.

24
00:01:38,467 --> 00:01:43,711
Preserve identifiers, original measurements, units and missing values.

25
00:01:44,167 --> 00:01:49,081
Track the relationship among processing, figures, text and versions.

26
00:01:49,535 --> 00:01:54,022
Clarify supplied materials and release scope in collaborative work.

27
00:01:54,482 --> 00:01:59,765
Keep the original numbers as well as final figures so the analysis can be revised.

28
00:02:00,219 --> 00:02:05,331
Consistent identifiers connect measurements to images and measurement paths.

29
00:02:05,785 --> 00:02:10,574
In collaborative work, record which materials were supplied by whom and the scope under

30
00:02:10,588 --> 00:02:13,452
which they can be shared or released.

31
00:02:14,255 --> 00:02:17,545
Use bilingual terms to search papers and data.

32
00:02:18,007 --> 00:02:22,955
Connect Nabunken records, tree-ring databases and primary papers.

33
00:02:23,415 --> 00:02:27,369
Continue testing explanations against observed material.

34
00:02:27,823 --> 00:02:32,981
The materials provide an entry to research literature and actual specimens.

35
00:02:33,441 --> 00:02:38,297
Bilingual search terms help connect local questions with studies elsewhere.

36
00:02:38,757 --> 00:02:43,876
After reading an explanation, return to the material and ask which visible or measurable

37
00:02:43,889 --> 00:02:46,360
feature would help test it.

38
00:02:47,163 --> 00:02:50,546
How do you distinguish a ring date from an event date?.

39
00:02:50,849 --> 00:02:54,652
Can you compare two explanations for one observation?.

40
00:02:54,955 --> 00:02:58,398
What new evidence could change your research conclusion?.

41
00:02:58,705 --> 00:03:03,540
Return to the opening question about why past dates and environments matter.

42
00:03:03,994 --> 00:03:08,119
What longer perspective can tree rings bring to a present question?

43
00:03:08,573 --> 00:03:13,192
Answer these three prompts and choose the part of your research plan that most needs further

44
00:03:13,205 --> 00:03:14,780
evidence.

45
00:03:15,583 --> 00:03:20,275
Record observations, verify dates and compare explanations.

46
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:24,138
Move among specimens, data and literature.

47
00:03:24,598 --> 00:03:28,072
Present a question together with the limits of its answer.

48
00:03:28,533 --> 00:03:33,217
Across the eight lessons we have moved from reading rings to combining several kinds of

49
00:03:33,230 --> 00:03:36,085
environmental and historical evidence.

50
00:03:36,546 --> 00:03:42,296
Careful observations, secure dating and explicit alternatives are useful far beyond this

51
00:03:42,309 --> 00:03:43,335
course.

52
00:03:43,796 --> 00:03:48,552
Apply them when reading new sources and developing questions of your own.
